# One camera's detection rows, chosen to sit ON the edge of the `tracker_options` seam.
#
# Both planes run the same ByteTrack over the same boxes; what differs is how each maps
# the tracker's answer back onto the detection ROWS.
#
#   C++  `TrackerShard::update` reads `Track::last_match` -- the row the pool recorded
#        when it applied the measurement. Exact provenance, no threshold.
#   PY   `elements/track.py::_attribute` re-derives it, by a one-to-one IoU assignment
#        between the track's FILTERED box and the frame's boxes, cut at `attribution_iou`
#        (default 0.3).
#
# Two mechanisms can separate them, and there is a scenario for each.
#
# THE THRESHOLD. A track whose posterior box overlaps its own measurement by less than
# the cut is dropped by Python and kept by C++. Reaching it means shrinking the box until
# the posterior lags -- but the tracker's OWN gate gives out first. `aspect_edge` is the
# narrowest width that still keeps the track (posterior IoU 0.3231 against a 0.30 cut) and
# `aspect_break` is one tenth of a pixel further, where the tracker re-mints instead. The
# pair brackets that edge, so a submodule bump that moves it changes this golden.
#
# THE ASSIGNMENT. Python's mapping is a global one-to-one assignment, so two tracks whose
# posterior boxes nearly coincide can be paired the other way round from the tracker's own
# matching -- no threshold involved. `crossing_near_tie` passes two boxes within 2px of
# each other, where the two pairings score within 0.18 of one another.
#
# `frame <n>` then one `box` per detection row: x1 y1 x2 y2 score

scenario steady
# The control: a box that does not move. Both planes must agree here, or the case is
# measuring the tracker rather than the attribution.
frame 0
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 1
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 2
box 100 100 140 200 0.90

scenario aspect_edge
# Six steady frames, then the NARROWEST final width that still keeps the track. Measured,
# not guessed: posterior IoU 0.3231 vs the 0.30 cut -- the closest a live track ever comes
# to being dropped by Python's re-derivation.
frame 0
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 1
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 2
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 3
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 4
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 5
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 6
box 100 100 112.4 200 0.90

scenario aspect_break
# The same, one tenth of a pixel narrower: the tracker's gate gives out, so it re-mints
# rather than dropping the row. This is the side of the edge the threshold never reaches.
frame 0
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 1
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 2
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 3
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 4
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 5
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
frame 6
box 100 100 112.3 200 0.90

scenario crossing_near_tie
# Two objects closing at 8px/frame and passing within 2px at frame 15, where the two
# possible pairings score within 0.18 of one another. The one mechanism that does not
# depend on a threshold: a global assignment is free to choose the other pairing.
frame 0
box 100 100 140 200 0.90
box 218 100 258 200 0.85
frame 1
box 104 100 144 200 0.90
box 214 100 254 200 0.85
